## Your five questions, answered

**Q2 — `--target` unexercised.** Now exercised. `--target 590`, `--dry-run` and the bare form all
enter `main()`, from three cwd spellings. 5/5. Every run used `127.0.0.1:1` supplied through
`env -i`; no run touched a real database.

**Q5 — symlinks reasoned, not constructed.** Now constructed. Symlinked directory (relative and
absolute) and symlinked *file*: all 3 enter `main()`. The `/tmp` → `/private/tmp` case enters with
the stack reading `at async main (/private/tmp/…)` — the `realpathSync` normalisation is
load-bearing and correct. *(My first attempt here reported a NO-OP. That was my own probe copying
the tree without `node_modules` so `pg` could not resolve. Re-run properly, it entered `main()`. I
corrected it before reporting, not after.)*

**Q4 — CI-runner behaviour inferred.** Materially narrowed. I ran both directions on **Node
v18.20.8** as well as v24.14.0. **The runner's Node 20 lies between two verified majors.** The
Node-18 import printed `IMPORTED_OK function` *and* no ECONNREFUSED — a positive paired with the
absence.

**Q1 — is absence-of-connect-attempt sufficient?** Yes, and it is not a vacuum, for two reasons:
my mutation shows test A *does* fail when the migrator runs, so the absence is produced by a
working detector; and test A pairs the absence with `toContain('IMPORTED_OK function')`, so the
module provably evaluated to completion — had the guard been true, `main()` would have run. The
builder's refusal to point a run at a real DB was right and I did not relax it either.

*Live proof you were right to ask:* my own first Node-18 probe printed "import did NOT run the
migrator" **purely because `timeout` was missing from my stripped PATH and nothing executed at
all.** An absence assertion without a paired positive is worthless. Test A has the pairing.

**Q3 — tsconfig excludes `scripts`.** Confirmed: `exclude: ["node_modules","scripts"]`. The
project typecheck never covered this file, before or after. Pre-existing gap, not a T11 defect —
recorded so nobody later reads `tsc -p tsconfig.json` passing as evidence about this file.

## One more angle you did not ask for, and it mattered

**Does CI actually run this test?** A guard CI never runs is a §N.8 non-detector. It does:
`ci.yml:172 npm test` → `vitest run` with no path filter; the `node` project declares no
`include`, so the default glob picks up `scripts/__tests__/`; not in `SHARED_EXCLUDE`; `tsx` is a
devDependency so the subprocess binary exists after `npm ci`. And a missing binary would fail
loudly — both tests assert positive content.

## RES-1 — the residual, and the one claim I am refuting

The builder's §6.1 says a wrongly-false guard fails **"loud"**. It does not.

- `deploy.yml:326–335` runs `npx tsx scripts/migrate.ts` with **no post-run assertion**.
- `main()`'s success path prints **nothing** when nothing is pending — `ran` is empty, so
  `ran.forEach(…)` emits zero lines, exit 0 (`migrate.ts:704–710`).
- So **"already up to date" and "the migrator never ran" are byte-identical in the CI log:
  nothing, exit 0** — and `deploy-web` then ships on `needs: [migrate]`.

That is CLAUDE.md §N.4's named hazard verbatim: *migrations silently doing nothing while the
deploy reports success*. The code comment at `migrate.ts:730` even contradicts itself — "exit
silently **and loudly** wrong."

**I am not asking for the direction to be reversed.** It is correct: a false positive migrates
production from an import; a false negative skips a deploy. But note the risk *profile* changed —
pre-T11 the deploy path's condition (`NODE_ENV !== 'test'`) was trivially true on the runner;
post-T11 it depends on a realpath comparison never executed on that platform. Angle 7 narrows that
to Node 20 specifically, bracketed on both sides.

The residual has **no detector**, which is §N.8. Cheapest honest fixes: (a) have `main()` print a
terminal sentinel unconditionally and grep for it in the deploy step, or (b) exit non-zero with a
diagnostic when not the entrypoint *and* `argv[1]` resolves to a file basenamed `migrate.ts`.

**Suggest a separate work item. Not a hold on the wave.**

